<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Permits unencrypted HTTP, which Android has blocked by default since API 28.

  This exists for one feature: a self-hosted AI connection. A model server on the
  user's own network is reached at something like http://192.168.1.5:8000, because
  vLLM, Ollama, LM Studio and llama.cpp all serve plain HTTP and there is no
  certificate a LAN address could present anyway — the address moves with the DHCP
  lease. Without this file that connection fails at the socket, before any request,
  with an error that says nothing about why.

  IT IS APP-WIDE BECAUSE IT CANNOT BE ANYTHING ELSE. A domain-config needs literal
  hostnames written here at build time, and the whole point of the field is that only
  the user knows the address. There is no runtime API to permit one host either:
  NetworkSecurityPolicy is a process-wide, read-only fact. So the choice was between
  this and making the commonest self-hosted setup unreachable without a reverse
  proxy, which is not a thing to ask of somebody who just wants their own model to
  answer a macro.

  Two consequences, both deliberate. `action.http` gains the same reach, which is a
  gain rather than a cost — a LAN endpoint was previously unreachable from it for the
  same invisible reason. And an AI connection *can* now be pointed at an unencrypted
  address across the internet, which would put an API key on the wire in the clear;
  the connection editor warns whenever the address starts with http:// (see
  AiBaseUrl.isCleartext), because only the user knows whether the far end is their
  own living room or somebody else's server.

  Everything else keeps the platform defaults: the system trust anchors are
  unchanged, and the Hue bridge's pinned certificate is handled in code
  (HueTransport) rather than here, since no static config can pin against an address
  that moves.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
